// Behavioral API documentation for the ROADWATCH backend. Static Markdown
// authored from the current backend source; it reads no runtime state.
mixin () {
  public query func getApiDoc() : async Text {
    "# ROADWATCH Backend API\n\nROADWATCH is an AI-assisted road-accident detection and emergency-response backend for a city traffic-surveillance control room. It stores CCTV cameras, vehicle-detection logs, potential-accident incidents, emergency alerts, per-camera monitoring sessions and detection settings, and derives dashboard statistics and analytics from those records.\n\n## Authentication and authorization\n\n- **No authentication is required.** Every endpoint, including all mutations (create/update/delete, monitoring control, monitoring-session control, frame reporting, detection recording, accident generation, status changes, settings, sample data), is callable by any caller, including anonymous visitors. The app presents these as normal user actions and offers no sign-in control.\n- The app's frontend pins an Internet Identity derivation origin, published at `/.well-known/ii-derivation-origin`. An agent already holding the user's Internet Identity authorization derives the correct per-app principal against that origin (for example `icp identity link web <name> --app <host>`); such a delegation acts with the user's full authority in this app until it expires. Because no endpoint consults identity, this derivation affects only the optional role calls below.\n- Read endpoints (`listCameras`, `listDetections`, `listIncidents`, `getIncident`, `listAlerts`, `getSettings`, `getDashboardStats`, `getAnalytics`, `getMonitoringSession`, `getFrameCount`, `getDetectionCount`) are open queries and return data to any caller.\n- The authorization mixin remains wired as infrastructure: `_initialize_access_control` registers the caller (first caller becomes admin, later callers become users), `getCallerUserRole` and `isCallerAdmin` report the caller's role, and `assignCallerUserRole` lets an admin assign roles. These role calls are optional and are not required to use any Roadwatch endpoint.\n\n## Units and encoding\n\n- All timestamps (`createdAt`, `detectedAt`, `raisedAt`, `lastSampleLoadedAt`, alert history `at`, monitoring-session `startedAt`/`updatedAt`) are **nanoseconds since the Unix epoch**, signed `Int` (`Time.now()`).\n- Identifiers (`CameraId`, `IncidentId`, `AlertId`, `LogId`) are `Nat` counters starting at 0. A monitoring session is keyed by its `cameraId`.\n- `latitude`/`longitude` are decimal degrees; `confidence`, `sensitivity` and `confidenceThreshold` are floats in `[0.0, 1.0]`.\n- `BoundingBox` coordinates are normalized to `[0.0, 1.0]` relative to the frame.\n- Optional fields (`uploadedFileRef`, `confidence`, `snapshotRef`, `lastSampleLoadedAt`) are Candid `opt`; `null` means absent.\n- Variants: `VideoSourceKind` = `uploadedFile | rtspStream | sample`; `ConnectionStatus` = `online | offline`; `MonitoringState` = `monitoring | stopped`; `DetectionSource` = `simulated | model`; `IncidentStatus` = `potential | verified | falseAlarm | resolved`; `VerificationStatus` = `unverified | verified | falseAlarm`; `ResolutionStatus` = `unresolved | resolved`; `NotificationStatus` = `pending | notified | failed`; `AlertStatus` = `active | acknowledged | resolved`; `VehicleClass` = `car | motorcycle | bus | truck`.\n- `MutationResult` is `variant { ok; err : RoadwatchError }`; `RoadwatchError` is `variant { notFound : Text; invalidInput : Text; notAuthorized }`.\n\n## Monitoring sessions\n\nA monitoring session is the backend's record of a camera being watched. The backend performs **no server-side video processing or model inference**: the browser decodes the uploaded video and reports the frame count it has processed, and the backend only stores that number. This is why frame counts are described as client-reported.\n\n- `startMonitoringSession(cameraId)` records `startedAt`, sets `active = true`, resets `frameCount` to 0, and sets the camera's `monitoringState` to `monitoring`. It **refuses an offline camera** with `#err(#invalidInput(\"Camera is offline and cannot be monitored\"))` and never marks an offline camera as monitoring. An unknown camera returns `#err(#notFound(\"Camera not found\"))`.\n- `stopMonitoringSession(cameraId)` sets `active = false`, keeps the final `frameCount`, and sets the camera's `monitoringState` to `stopped`. A camera with no session returns `#err(#notFound(\"Monitoring session not found\"))`.\n- `getMonitoringSession(cameraId)` returns the stored session, or `null` when none has been started.\n- `reportFrameCount(cameraId, frameCount)` stores the client-reported frame count and updates `updatedAt`. It requires an existing session and returns `#err(#notFound(\"Monitoring session not found\"))` otherwise.\n- `getFrameCount(cameraId)` returns the stored frame count, or 0 when no session exists.\n- `getDetectionCount(cameraId)` returns the number of stored detection-log entries for that camera.\n- The legacy `startMonitoring`/`stopMonitoring` endpoints remain and are kept consistent with session state: `startMonitoring` sets `monitoringState = monitoring` and `connectionStatus = online`; `stopMonitoring` sets `monitoringState = stopped`.\n\n## Lifecycle and polling\n\n- A session begins with `startMonitoringSession` and ends with `stopMonitoringSession`. While active, the client polls `getFrameCount`/`getMonitoringSession` and pushes progress with `reportFrameCount`.\n- `getMonitoringSession` and `getFrameCount` are queries, so they are cheap to poll; `reportFrameCount` is an update and should be called at a bounded cadence (for example once per second), not per video frame.\n- A camera is shown as monitoring only while its session is `active`; a disconnected (offline) camera cannot start a session.\n\n## Mutation retry safety\n\n- `startMonitoringSession` is **not idempotent**: each call resets `frameCount` to 0 and restamps `startedAt`. Retrying it discards the current count.\n- `reportFrameCount` is idempotent for a given value: re-sending the same count leaves the same stored value (only `updatedAt` advances).\n- `stopMonitoringSession` is idempotent in effect: stopping an already-stopped session keeps the final count.\n- `generateAccidentEvent` is **not idempotent**: every call creates a new incident and a new emergency alert. Do not retry it blindly.\n- `recordDetection` appends a new log entry on every call; retrying duplicates the entry.\n- `loadSampleData` appends a fresh sample set on every call.\n\n## Errors and gotchas\n\n- Mutations return `MutationResult`; check the `#err` arm rather than assuming success. `#notFound` and `#invalidInput` carry a human-readable message; `#notAuthorized` is never produced by the current endpoints.\n- `createCamera` assigns the camera `code` server-side as `CAM-<id>` and defaults `connectionStatus = online`, `monitoringState = stopped`.\n- `generateAccidentEvent` copies the camera's id, code, road name, area, latitude, longitude and the detection timestamp onto the incident, then raises the matching emergency alert with `location = \"<roadName>, <area>\"`. The snapshot survives a later camera edit or delete.\n- `recordDetection` rejects a `confidence` outside `[0.0, 1.0]` with `#invalidInput`. The `simulated` flag distinguishes simulated pipeline output from real model output; the backend never claims model accuracy.\n- `updateSettings` rejects `sensitivity` or `confidenceThreshold` outside `[0.0, 1.0]`.\n- `updateCamera` preserves server-owned fields (`code`, `connectionStatus`, `monitoringState`, `createdAt`); only caller-editable details change.\n- The OQL `schema()`/`execute()` endpoints expose cameras, incidents, alerts, detection logs and monitoring sessions to the Data Intelligence agent at controller-only authorization.";
  };
};
